Title: The Ledge
Order: 7
Production Code: 1x07

EXT. BROWN'S CAMP - NIGHT

PAGE: 1

PANEL: A messenger on a lathered horse stumbling into camp, left arm hanging useless, white as death; Maybee catches him.

MESSENGER
Rangers. Three hundred around the old farmhouse. Judah says he can hold them off until daybreak.

PANEL: Brown, transformed, his eyes like coals.

BROWN
Two hours after midnight. The boy knows his business.

PAGE: 2

PANEL: The women hurrying up the mountainside to the cave, a rifle in every hand; Winona in front with a rifle at her hip, commanding the home-guard.

CAPTION: Winona was in command of the home-guard.

PANEL: Warren in the saddle among a hundred riders, looking back at the dark mountain; at the top a slight figure with a rifle waves a farewell salute.

PANEL: Dawn. Riders in a long column on a track in the grey light. Maybee falls back to Warren and points to the left, where another cloud of dust is rising.

MAYBEE
Reinforcements. They're the boys Reynolds has collected to help us.

PANEL: Maybee, amazed, shading his eyes with a hand.

MAYBEE (CONT'D)
But who's that riding beside Reynolds? Dog my cats, may I be teetotally smashed ef it don't look like Parson Steward!

PAGE: 3

PANEL: The two columns meeting; men cheering; Steward riding into the midst of the column and gripping hands all round; Warren struck dumb.

STEWARD
Praise God from whom all blessings flow.

WARREN
Amen.

PANEL: A flat view of the farmhouse far off, smoke on the walls, riflemen behind trees; Brown, Steward and Maybee reining in on a slope.

PANEL: Brown, with Warren, drawing him to the flank.

BROWN
My boy, you are going under fire. Shake hands; fire low; look well to the hinder side of your rifle. God bless you!

PANEL: Maybee riding behind the men, a frenzy in his eyes.

MAYBEE
Give 'em ----, boys! Remember our friends they've butchered, and our women and little children.

PANEL: The order to charge; a hundred riders loose on the plain, yelling.

SFX: YAAAAAAH

PAGE: 4

PANEL: A wild scene: smoke, flashes, horses rearing, men on the ground. Warren off his horse in hand-to-hand struggle with Gideon Holmes, whose long fingers are sunk into his throat.

SFX: KRAK KRAK KRAK

PANEL: Warren brings his pistol butt down on Holmes's face; again; again; the blows blind and ungoverned.

PANEL: Warren staring down at the limp body under him; his hand stops.

PANEL: In the smoke, Brown, Steward and Maybee fighting side by side, Steward bellowing verses.

STEWARD
Behold, the uncircumcised Philistine, how he defies the armies of the living God!

PANEL: A column of anti-slavery men advancing singing hymns and praising God against the Rangers' drunken rush; the Rangers breaking.

CAPTION: Like the old Spartans, the anti-slavery men advanced singing hymns.

PAGE: 5

PANEL: The flying Rangers: some toward the hills. Among them, spurring his horse across the plain, Thomson abandoning his horse at the foot of the rising ground. In the distance on the plain a rider pursues another: Judah on a Ranger horse running down a man in a Colonel's coat; the Colonel falls.

CAPTION: Thomson saw Col. Titus pursued by Judah, speeding over the plain; he saw them meet and the Colonel fall.

PANEL: Judah turning from the body and slowly beginning to climb the mountain.

PANEL: Thomson crawling and creeping over stones and through bushes, higher and higher; a ledge, a crevice between two giant rocks; he lies there breathing hard.

PANEL: Far below, the Possawatomie, a silver thread, sixty feet down; below the ledge a jutting shelf ten feet down, and giant steps to the river.

PANEL: Thomson on the shelf, rifle strapped to him, turning to begin the descent.

THOMSON
Lucky once more. That black demon has missed again.

PAGE: 6

PANEL: Thomson turns and faces Judah in the shadow of a great boulder, a smile on his face, rifle up.

JUDAH
Stop where you are!

PANEL: Thomson's hand reaching for his rifle; Judah's rifle pointed up along its barrel.

JUDAH (CONT'D)
Hands up!

PANEL: Thomson's hands go up obediently; the morning sun on the mountain; birds feeding their young; bees; a murmur of the stream far below; all peace.

CAPTION: But within two human hearts surged the wild passions of fierce animals at bay.

PANEL: Thomson on the narrow ledge with the clouds of heaven at his back; his head throbbing; his eyes darting; his heart sick with terror.

THOMSON
I suppose I am your prisoner? I am to be treated as a prisoner of war?

PANEL: Judah's terrible smile, calm and dispassionate.

JUDAH
Would you have treated me as a prisoner of war if you had captured me?

PAGE: 7

PANEL: Thomson's lips move; the word breaks involuntarily out of him.

THOMSON
No.

PANEL: Judah, one step closer.

JUDAH
Very well.

PANEL: Thomson, frantic.

THOMSON
I demand to be taken before Captain Brown.

THOMSON (CONT'D)
Surely he is human; he will not give me into the hands of a savage to be tortured!

PANEL: Thomson, fawning, his grin like a trapped rat's.

THOMSON (CONT'D)
Judah, I own I done you dirt mean. I ask yer pardon.

THOMSON (CONT'D)
I can tell you a heap of things worth more'n my life to the gal.

PANEL: Judah, untouched.

JUDAH
You stole Winona's liberty and mine. I know what your promises are worth.

PAGE: 8 (SPLASH)

PANEL: Full-page splash: the mountain ledge seen from below against a brilliant sky, a tiny figure in the middle at the edge of the cliff, arms raised, mid-leap, coat flying; above him on the rock, a tall dark silhouette with a rifle. A sheer drop to the glinting thread of the river. The rocks, the sun, the clouds.
LIGHT: Hard morning sun from behind the rocks

JUDAH
I've sworn to kill you and I intend to keep my oath.

JUDAH (CONT'D)
When I count three, jump backwards, or I put a bullet into your miserable carcass.

CAPTION: One! Two! Three!

SFX: AAAAAAAAH

PAGE: 9

PANEL: Judah standing very still at the edge, listening: the thud of the falling body, the swish of displaced bushes, the rattle of gravel.

SFX: thud thud thud

PANEL: A last awful scream rising from the valley, echoing and re-echoing along the hillside.

PANEL: Judah steps to the brink and looks over, then draws back.

PANEL: Judah leaning on his gun, in the sun, a smile spreading and fading.

CAPTION: To him his recent act was one of simple justice.

PANEL: Close on Judah's face; the smile slipping; a new thought entering his eyes.

CAPTION: What if a thought just born in his mind should prove true? O, to be free once more!

PANEL: A rustle of leaves; out from the shadow of the trees file a number of anti-slavery men led by Captain Brown and Parson Steward.

BROWN
Well, Judah, we've been watching your little drama. You promised to kill him and you've done it.

PAGE: 10

PANEL: Judah, fierce and open before them all.

JUDAH
Boys, I leave it to you if I'm not right in ridding the world of such a beast as Thomson.

PANEL: The men cheer so that it echoes and re-echoes among the hills.

PANEL: Steward, the same calm, unruffled front as when we first met him, a little paler; Judah wringing his hand in both of his.

JUDAH (CONT'D)
No wonder we have won, Parson; I heard them cry: "Look at the Parson!" and then they fled in every direction.

PANEL: Brown, gently ironic.

BROWN
Perhaps you'll be good enough to tell me where you come from, Parson. You've been dead to us for weeks past.

PANEL: Steward tells it, stirring his hat on a finger.

STEWARD
Oh, I been pretty near you right along. I was filled with shot and left for dead.

STEWARD (CONT'D)
Reynolds toted me on his back to his house and hid me in his loft.

PANEL: Steward at ease; the men doffing hats.

STEWARD (CONT'D)
There I lay eight long weeks.

STEWARD (CONT'D)
And here I am right side up with care, and the old woman and kids safe and hearty in your camp.

PAGE: 11

PANEL: Judah's face in profile as the men turn and go up the mountain path toward the cave.

BROWN
All's well that ends well.

PANEL: The men climbing the path through the sun; far above them, the cave mouth, with the women and rifles outside it.

PANEL: Judah alone on the ledge, looking down. He starts down the mountain path, impelled by a morbid fascination, toward the bed of the stream.

CAPTION: Impelled by a morbid fascination, Judah climbed down the mountain path seeking the bed of the stream below where lay the body of his foe.

EXT. THE POSSAWATOMIE CLIFFS, THE CAVE - DAY

PAGE: 12

PANEL: The same mountain, a different side. Winona patrols her beat at the mouth of the cave with her rifle at the trail, listening to the distant sound of firing that the breeze carries to her ears from time to time. She has no field-glass; figures on the plain are too small to tell friend from foe.

CAPTION: All through the long morning Winona patrolled her beat listening with anxious heart.

PANEL: Noon. One of Captain Brown's daughters brings her a tin cup of coffee, the only break in her solitary vigil. Winona drinks it standing, never taking her eyes off the horizon.

PANEL: Late afternoon shadows lengthening across the hills; Winona's mind pictures flames leaping from the woods, little children under merciless hoofs.

CAPTION: She wondered how the battle went in sickening dread, and then rebuked her own impatience for news.

PANEL: Winona sets down her rifle against a rock and goes down the hillside toward the stream; a stern face.

CAPTION: She determined to risk all in an endeavor to obtain news of her friends, of Warren.

PANEL: She follows the stream bed in the shadow of the cliff, small against the rock wall above her, the sun beating on its upper face. A silver thread of water over pebbles.

PAGE: 13

PANEL: Far above her head: a single gunshot, a scream of agony tearing through space and echoing down the cliff face.

SFX: KRAK

PANEL: Winona covers her face with her hands a moment, then drops them in time to see a falling body drop into the water almost at her feet.

SFX: SPLASH

PANEL: She stands rigid. The soft waves steal up and fling themselves over the quiet body huddled breast-high in the stream.

PANEL: Winona on her knees on the bank, a thought crossing her face like a blow.

WINONA
If it should be Warren!

PANEL: She takes the unconscious head in both hands and turns the face toward her: the sightless eyes of THOMSON, mud and blood across his forehead.

PANEL: Winona sobs aloud in relief, then, gathering herself, gives broken thanks that it was not Warren. She looks about her for means to help the man.

CAPTION: He was her enemy, but the mother instinct that dwells in all good women gave her calmness and strength to do, and the heart to forgive.

PAGE: 14

PANEL: Winona turns for help and faces Judah coming out from the trees with his rifle.

WINONA
Oh, Judah, he is alive!

PANEL: Judah stares at her and at the figure in the water.

JUDAH
What! Not dead yet? I thought I had settled his case for all time.

PANEL: Winona stern, catching the ferocious light that still glimmers in his eyes as he lifts his gun to the hollow of his arm.

WINONA
Help me to carry this man to camp; surely you are satisfied now. You cannot shoot a dying man.

PANEL: Judah, reproachfully.

JUDAH
I did it for you as much as for myself. Have you forgotten your father?

PANEL: Winona, with her hand on his sleeve.

WINONA
I do not forget. God forbid! But you have done enough.

PAGE: 15 (TURN)

PANEL: Judah, hard, shaking his head.

JUDAH
Not enough. He is the hater of my race. I made an oath; it was no idle threat.

PANEL: He poises the gun. Quick as a flash the girl throws herself in front of the unconscious Thomson.

WINONA
You shall not!

PANEL: Winona between the two, arms spread, chin up, her face blazing.

WINONA (CONT'D)
You make yourself as vile as the vilest of them, our enemies. Let the man die in peace. See, he is almost gone.

PANEL: A figure steps out from the trees behind: Warren, powder-black, his sleeve torn, one hand still holding a pistol.

WARREN
Yes, Judah, it is enough; she is right. Surely you must be sick of bloodshed.

PANEL: Winona turns and is folded in her lover's arms; Judah watches silently, then lowers his rifle.

JUDAH
Let it be as you wish.

PAGE: 16

PANEL: Judah and Warren lift Thomson from the stream and turn faint at the sight of his wounds.

WARREN
His back is broken.

PANEL: Judah, quietly, the sheer human repulsion mastering even him; the three of them carrying the body slowly up the steep ascent.

JUDAH
It were more merciful to shoot him on the spot.

PANEL: Wide on the plain below: dead men and horses and a few Rangers sitting with their hands tied; Brown's men moving among them with rifles.

CAPTION: The Rangers were completely routed by the desperate valor of the Brown men.

CAPTION: A number of prisoners were taken, who were to be tried by court-martial and shot, according to the rough justice of the times.

PANEL: In the camp: the wounded laid in a row on straw, Maybee among them, grey-faced, with a bandage round his body.

PAGE: 17

INT. BROWN'S CAMP, A HUT - DAWN

PANEL: Sunrise. Maybee wakes from a stupor in a bed of straw. Steward is at his side with a basin.

SFX: KRAKRAKRAKRAK

PANEL: Maybee, sitting up, stunned.

MAYBEE
What does that mean?

PANEL: Steward, calm, wringing a cloth.

STEWARD
Well, partner, you know we won the fight. Captain Brown is a shootin' the pris'ners.

PANEL: Maybee, wry, a hand on his chest.

MAYBEE
Well, now, ain't that tough fer a prefesser?

PANEL: Steward, quietly.

STEWARD
Not all the prisoners. The most of them have been begged off by young Maxwell.

STEWARD (CONT'D)
He's the most softest hearted young feller I ever met for such a good shot.

PANEL: Steward, gruffly, choking, sitting at the bedside.

STEWARD (CONT'D)
You ain't goin' nowhere, partner; we'll pull you through.

PAGE: 18

PANEL: Maybee, blinking, a wink planted in vacancy; Steward frowning hard to keep back the tears.

MAYBEE
We've been partners fer a right smart spell, ain't we, Parson?

STEWARD
You're a man to tie to, Maybee.

PANEL: Maybee, sputtering and giving way.

MAYBEE
D-- ye, Parson, don't make a baby er me. What's come o' Thomson an' the colonel?

PANEL: Steward, flat.

STEWARD
Devil's got the colonel and he's waiting fer Thomson. We've got him with a broken back next door to this house. Judah did it.

PANEL: One of the men at the door with a message for the Parson.

MESSENGER
Thomson is conscious, and going fast. He wants the Parson and Winona.

PANEL: Through the wall's open door: Thomson laid on a cot in the next hut, paralyzed in every limb, his head up, his eyes open and sane; Warren standing beside him.

PANEL: Close on Thomson's mouth, jokingly, to Warren.

THOMSON
How long do you give me, doc?

WARREN
Until it touches the heart.

PANEL: Thomson turns his head slightly, hearing it; a pause, a long breath; the sound of the volley dies away.

CAPTION: Thomson appeared to be thinking.

THOMSON
I got to own up.
